"# Appendix J — The Six Teaching Shapes

Made by Alfred Borden.

Every appendix so far taught you an *algorithm*. This one teaches a
*meta-skill*: how to turn a single verb into a lesson someone else can learn
from. There are six shapes for doing that, and to make the shapes legible we
hold the verb fixed — `fold`, SRFI-1's element-first reduction — and vary only
the shape. Watch the shape change while the subject stays still. Every snippet
below runs; the interpreter re-grades each one, so the code cannot drift out
from under the prose.

The six shapes, and the question each one answers:

| # | Shape | Question |
|---|-------|----------|
| 0 | verb-identity | *What is this verb across the whole grid?* |
| 1 | run-and-explain | *What happens, step by step?* |
| 2 | decompose-and-label | *What are the parts, and their names?* |
| 3 | build-up ladder | *How do I climb to it, one runnable rung at a time?* |
| 4 | reversals | *Can I go backward — words↔code, broken↔fixed?* |
| 5 | comprehension-vocabulary | *What is the vocabulary that makes it sayable?* |

## Shape 0 — verb-identity: the whole grid in one cell

A verb's identity is its full grid — the same shape realized in engineering, in
math, in computer science. A learner shown one cell can grow it outward into
*what is `fold`, really?* One cell: fold `+` over a list from seed `0`.

```scheme
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4))
;=> 10
```

That `10` is a doorway to three heights of the same idea. To a beginner it is a
machine that eats a list and returns one value. To a working programmer it is
`reduce` over a sequence. To a theorist it is the catamorphism — the universal
fold `(α × β → β) × β × [α] → β` that every other list reduction specializes.
One runnable seed, three altitudes.

## Shape 1 — run-and-explain: watch the answer come to be

Predict, then run, then trace the run. Fold `cons` from the empty list:

```scheme
(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))
;=> (5 4 3 2 1)
```

Reversed — and the reason *is* the lesson. SRFI-1's `fold` is element-first: the
combiner receives each element *before* the accumulator. So `(cons 1 '())` is
`(1)`, `(cons 2 '(1))` is `(2 1)`, and each element is pushed onto the front as
it arrives, yielding `(5 4 3 2 1)`. This is the classic identity `fold cons =
reverse` — obvious once you trace it, opaque until you do.

## Shape 2 — decompose-and-label: name the parts, then swap one

`fold` has three arguments: a *combiner*, a *seed*, and a *list*. Name them and
a deliberate substitution becomes possible:

```scheme
(define (sum xs) (fold + 0 xs))
(sum (list 1 2 3 4))
;=> 10
```

The signature is `(list-of number) -> number`. The inventory is combiner `+`,
seed `0`, list `xs`. The template is `(fold <combiner> <seed> <list>)`. Once the
template is visible you can grow a different organ from the same skeleton: keep
the template, swap `+` → `cons` and `0` → `'()`, and `sum` becomes `reverse`.
This is HtDP-style decomposition — signature, purpose, inventory, template —
applied to a single higher-order verb.

## Shape 3 — build-up ladder: one runnable rung at a time

You need not hold the whole program in your head. Climb it. Each rung runs on
its own and adds exactly one idea: generate → filter → reduce → abstract.

```scheme
(iota 6)
;=> (0 1 2 3 4 5)
```

```scheme
(filter odd? (iota 6))
;=> (1 3 5)
```

```scheme
(fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota 6)))
;=> 9
```

```scheme
(define (sum-odd n) (fold + 0 (filter odd? (iota n))))
(sum-odd 10)
;=> 25
```

Generate a range, filter to the odds, reduce the survivors to a sum, then
abstract the whole pipeline over `n`. This is the map-filter-fold triad — the
backbone of functional data processing — assembled one gradeable step at a time.

## Shape 4 — reversals: words↔code, broken↔fixed

Two directions most curricula skip. First, description to code — \"sum the
squares of 1 through 5\":

```scheme
(fold + 0 (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (iota 5 1)))
;=> 55
```

Second, broken to fixed — and some seeds **must fail**, because a failure for
the right reason is a lesson. Motoi's standard is R7RS-small + SRFI-1; it does
*not* bind R6RS/Racket's accumulator-first `fold-left`:

```text
(fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3))
;=> error: unbound symbol: fold-left
```

That refusal is deliberate — the interpreter is a free correctness gate against
the wrong library's name. The fix is the SRFI-1 verb Motoi *does* carry:

```scheme
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3))
;=> 6
```

A second, different break: `filter` wants a *predicate* first, not a value —

```text
(filter 42 (iota 6))
;=> error: not a function: 42
```

— and the error names the mistake exactly. `42` is not a function, so `filter`
cannot apply it. Give it a real predicate:

```scheme
(filter even? (iota 6))
;=> (0 2 4)
```

Reversibility — running the arrow both ways, and treating a right-reason error
as signal — is where a student stops transcribing and starts reasoning.

## Shape 5 — comprehension-vocabulary: the words for the pieces

The final shape gives the other five their language. Name the pieces and the
shape becomes sayable — askable, teachable. A fold with a filter inside it:

```scheme
(fold + 0 (filter odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
;=> 9
```

Point and name: `+` is the **combiner**; `0` is the **seed**; `odd?` is the
**predicate**; the whole expression is **pure** (no effect, identical result on
every run); and `fold` recurs in **tail position**, so it processes an arbitrarily
long list in constant stack space — where its cousin `fold-right` does not.
Combiner, seed, predicate, pure, tail-position: five words, and every reduction
in this book now shares one interface you can speak about out loud.

---

Six shapes, one verb, every block runnable. Each is a *seed*: hand it to a
learner and it grows into a whole conversation — which is exactly what a
teaching shape is for."
)
